# tools/ales_stereo_bell_witness.rish — stereo_bell: the parametric bell (peaking EQ) carried into stereo, the same two cutoffs and one gain on both channels — the fifth rung of the stereo EQ / filter class (ALES174).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES174.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-080752_fill-ales174-lotus-stereo-bell.md
# Stands on: lotus/bell.rye (ALES48 — the mono parametric bell run once per channel, its BellError reused whole), lotus/notch.rye (ALES47 — the zero-gain equality target, carried into stereo at ALES173), lotus/tone.rye (ALES40 —
# the low-pass/high-pass edges and the precheck sharing one validation), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and the one true saturate the bell's sum reuses).
#
# lotus/stereo_bell.rye turns a named band on a stereo master: it runs ALES48's proven mono bell on BOTH channels with the SAME two cutoffs and gain — keep the low and high bands at unity and scale the mid band, the exact
# residual M = x − L − H, by the gain, writing the sum saturated once. Unity gain reconstructs the input on both; zero gain is exactly the stereo notch on both; a settled constant survives on both (the mid band is zero at direct
# current, so the bell turns only the middle). A bell's cutoffs and gain are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the stereo image for free; each
# channel bells its own content, and like the shelf and notch and unlike the band-pass there is no carried state to thread. It validates the two cutoffs, the gain denominator, and the span once against the shared length before
# either channel is mutated (BadCoeff on an illegal cutoff, BadGain on a zero gain denominator, BadRange on a span past the samples), so a refusal never bells one channel and leaves the other torn; BellError reused whole. Values
# only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is four laws: the stereo bell law (each channel equal to ALES48's mono bell with the same knobs byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing), the three-gain
# law (unity the identity on both, zero equal to the stereo notch on both, a settled constant surviving per channel), the image law (an identical-channel master staying identical through the bell), and the balance / silence /
# atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_bell_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-bell: Language EN — stereo_bell turns a named band on a stereo master by running ALES48's proven mono bell on both channels with the same two cutoffs and gain: keep the low and high bands at unity and scale the mid band, the exact residual M equals x minus L minus H, by the gain, writing the sum saturated once. Unity gain reconstructs the input on both; zero gain is exactly the stereo notch on both; a settled constant survives on both, since the mid band is zero at direct current so the bell turns only the middle. A bell's cutoffs and gain are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the stereo image for free; each channel bells its own content, and like the shelf and notch and unlike the band-pass there is no carried state to thread. It validates the two cutoffs, the gain denominator, and the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never bells one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-bell: Style Radiant — the stereo EQ and filter class has its tone control (ALES170), shelf (ALES171), band-pass (ALES172, isolate the middle), and notch (ALES173, drop the middle); the gesture a mixer reaches for more than any other is the bell — turn the middle band by a chosen gain while the edges stay exactly as they were. Lift a vocal's presence, tame a boxy room mode, dig out a kick's thump. ALES48 built that for a mono clip on the tone stack's lesson: the middle is the exact residual M equals x minus L minus H, so gaining only the middle can never disturb the edges, and unity gain reconstructs the input by construction. The bell is the whole fixed-equalization family at three gains — unity the identity, zero the notch, a larger gain a boost. This rung carries it into stereo the way the class asks — run ALES48's proven bell on each channel with the same cutoffs and gain, so the turned band lifts on both speakers together. Like every parametric edit, a bell's cutoffs and gain are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the stereo image for free, with no linking and no measurement; each channel bells its own content, which is exactly right for equalization — an identical-channel, mono-in-stereo master stays identical because the same input through the same knobs is the same deterministic output, while a panned master shapes each side by its own spectrum. Like the shelf and notch and unlike the band-pass, there is no carried state to lift: the bell runs fresh filters over the whole span each call, so a stereo bell is a stateless per-channel pass with nothing to thread across a seam. The crux is four laws: the stereo bell law (each channel equal to ALES48's mono bell with the same cutoffs and gain byte for byte, the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed); the three-gain law (unity gain reconstructing the input on both, zero gain equal to ALES173's stereo notch on both, and a settled constant surviving on both at any gain as the mid band is zero at direct current); the image law (an identical-channel master staying identical through the bell, the shared knobs holding the image); and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law (an all-silent master belling to silence on both, the lengths held, an illegal low cutoff, an illegal high cutoff, a zero gain denominator, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count zero the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-bell: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each belled through ALES48's own residual and scratch bands, every band scale and sum run in i64 so nothing overflows before the single clamp per channel; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a bell writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the two cutoffs, the gain denominator, and the span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never bells one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoBellError is ALES48's BellError (BadCoeff, BadGain, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault. The stereo bell law is proven by running both stereo_bell and ALES48's mono bell on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the three-gain law proves unity gain is the input byte for byte on both, zero gain equals ALES173's stereo notch on both, and a settled constant is untouched at a boosting gain on each channel; the image law proves an identical-channel master stays identical through the bell; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the cutoffs over sample indices rather than crossovers in hertz, the gain a plain fraction rather than decibels, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_bell"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_bell.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-bell: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_bell.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-bell: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-bell: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-bell' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-bell: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-bell line"
